  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「ミリキア・エクスケルサ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%9F%E3%83%AA%E3%82%AD%E3%82%A2%E3%83%BB%E3%82%A8%E3%82%AF%E3%82%B9%E3%82%B1%E3%83%AB%E3%82%B5",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 109582750,
      "text": "ミリキア・エクスケルサ とは、クワ科ミリキア属の樹木の一種である。シノニムの Chlorophora excelsa (Welw.) Benth. & Hook.f. で言及した文献も複数存在し、イロコ（iroko）という木材が得られる。自生地はアフリカ諸国の広範囲にわたる が、東アフリカでは伐採によって個体数が激減し絶滅の懸念が生じている。西アフリカでは聖なる木あるいは精霊の宿る木と信じられている。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Milicia excelsa」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milicia_excelsa",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1374396655,
      "text": "Milicia excelsa is a tree species from the genus Milicia of the family Moraceae. Distributed across tropical Central Africa, it is one of two species yielding timber commonly known as ọjị, African teak, iroko, intule, kambala, moreira, mvule, odum and tule. The tree has several medicinal applications and is considered sacred in parts of West Africa. It is currently listed as \"near-threatened\" by the International Union for Conservation of Nature."
    }
  },
